  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "For a permutation PPP of size NNN, and an integer k&nbsp;(1≤k≤N)k \\ (1 \\le k \\le N)k&nbsp;(1≤k≤N), define f(P,k)f(P, k)f(P,k) as ∑i=1N−k+1max⁡(Pi,Pi+1,Pi+2,…,Pi+k−1)\\displaystyle{\\sum_{i = 1}^{N - k + 1} \\max(P_i, P_{i + 1}, P_{i + 2}, \\ldots, P_{i + k - 1})}i=1∑N−k+1​max(Pi​,Pi+1​,Pi+2​,…,Pi+k−1​), i.e. the sum of maximum elements in all kkk - sized subarrays.\n\nLet g(N,k)g(N, k)g(N,k) denote the number of permutations PPP of size NNN which have the maximum value of f(P,k)f(P, k)f(P,k).\n\nGiven an integer NNN, find the value of g(N,k)g(N, k)g(N,k) for each 1≤k≤N1\\le k \\le N1≤k≤N.\n\nSince the value can be huge, print it modulo 109+710^9 + 7109+7.\n\nNote that a permutation of size NNN consists of all elements from 111 to NNN exactly once.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of a single integer NNN - the size of the permutation.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line, NNN space-separated integers denoting the value of g(N,k)g(N, k)g(N,k) for each 1≤k≤N1\\le k \\le N1≤k≤N, modulo 109+710^9 + 7109+7.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤5001 \\le T \\le 5001≤T≤500",
      "1≤N≤3⋅1051 \\le N \\le 3 \\cdot 10^51≤N≤3⋅105",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 3⋅1053 \\cdot 10^53⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n1\n2\n3",
        "output": "1\n2 2\n6 2 6",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: There is only one possible permutation of size 111. Thus, f([1],1)=1f([1], 1) = 1f([1],1)=1 and g(1,1)=1g(1, 1) = 1g(1,1)=1.\nTest case 222:\n\ng(2,1)=2g(2, 1) = 2g(2,1)=2 as there are two permutations with maximum f(P,1)f(P, 1)f(P,1)\n\nf([1,2],1)=1+2=3f([1,2],1) = 1+2 = 3f([1,2],1)=1+2=3\nf([2,1],1)=2+1=3f([2,1],1) = 2+1 = 3f([2,1],1)=2+1=3\n\n\ng(2,2)=2g(2, 2) = 2g(2,2)=2 as there are two permutations with maximum f(P,2)f(P, 2)f(P,2)\n\nf([1,2],2)=2f([1,2],2) = 2f([1,2],2)=2\nf([2,1],2)=2f([2,1],2) = 2f([2,1],2)=2\n\n\n\nTest case 333: For k=1k = 1k=1 and k=3k = 3k=3, all permutations have the same value of f(P,k)f(P, k)f(P,k), thus the answer is 666. However, for k=2k = 2k=2, the optimal permutations are [2,3,1][2, 3, 1][2,3,1] and [1,3,2][1, 3, 2][1,3,2] only."
      }
    ]
